--- ANSWER ---
The practical answer is: a CLFN can be created and configured through VI Scripting; copying a donor is not technically mandatory. However, `Parameter Info` is only partly documented, its numeric enum encodings appear undocumented, and the robust production solution is usually to copy a hand-configured donor CLFN and change only its simple properties.

## 1. Creating the CLFN

`New VI Object` accepts the specific VI Server class:

```text
Generic
  GObject
    Node
      GrowableFunction
        CallLibrary
```

The CallLibrary class ID is `16417`. It is a VI Scripting class and exposes the library, function, calling-convention, threading, and parameter properties. [LabVIEW Wiki: CallLibrary class](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/CallLibrary_class)

There is also evidence that the normal object-style name is exactly:

```text
Call Library Function Node
```

A public LabVIEW scripting project lists that string among the accepted `New VI Object` object names. [CalmyJane/labview_assistant on GitHub](https://github.com/CalmyJane/labview_assistant)

More strongly, an old working scripting example explicitly says it created the CLFN with `New VI Object`, using a `Node ??GrowableFunction ??CallLibrary` class constant, and subsequently configured the node. Its author published `CLFN_per_Skript.zip` as a working LabVIEW 2010+ implementation. [LabVIEWForum.de example and discussion](https://www.labviewforum.de/Thread-Automatische-Konfiguration-eines-Knotens-zum-Aufruf-externer-Bibliothekn-CLF)

NI also published a downloadable example named ?�Scripting a Call Library Function Node Using LabVIEW?? its description says it creates and configures a CLFN which calls a Windows API function. [NI Community code example](https://forums.ni.com/t5/Example-Code/Scripting-a-Call-Library-Function-Node-Using-LabVIEW/ta-p/3514624)

Therefore:

- **Verified:** direct creation is possible.
- **Best candidate style string:** `Call Library Function Node`.
- **Best class input:** `Node ??GrowableFunction ??CallLibrary`.
- **Not verified externally:** the underlying numeric U32 value of that style-ring item in LabVIEW 2026.

If direct dropping proves version-sensitive, copying from a donor is a standard scripting workaround. NI-community experts specifically recommend copying objects when a desired `New VI Object` style is missing or difficult to identify. [NI forum: New VI Object style discussion](https://forums.ni.com/t5/LabVIEW/VI-Scripting-Input-for-style-in-quot-New-VI-Object-quot-function/td-p/3267356)

## 2. CLFN properties and `Parameter Info`

The directly useful CallLibrary properties are:

| Property | ID | Type | Verified value/meaning |
|---|---:|---|---|
| Library Path | `636D400` | Path | DLL/shared-library path |
| Function Name | `636D401` | String | Exported function name |
| Calling Convention | `636D402` | U32 | `0 = C`, `1 = stdcall (WINAPI)` |
| Any Thread? | `636D403` | Boolean | `TRUE = Run in any thread` |
| Parameter Info | `636D409` | Array of Common Cluster | Return value followed by arguments |

These are listed in the [CallLibrary class reference](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/CallLibrary_class). The specific property pages confirm [Library Path](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/CallLibrary_class/Library_Path_property), [Function Name](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/CallLibrary_class/Function_Name_property), [Calling Convention values](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/CallLibrary_class/Calling_Convention_property), and the Boolean [Any Thread?](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/CallLibrary_class/Any_Thread%3F_property).

Thus the non-parameter configuration should be straightforward:

```text
Library Path       = <DLL path>
Function Name      = <export name>
Calling Convention = 0
Any Thread?        = TRUE
```

NI?�s documentation says ?�Run in any thread??keeps the call in its current execution thread and must only be selected for thread-safe library code. [NI-derived CLFN configuration documentation](https://documentation.help/Call-Library-Function-Node/Configuring_the_CLF_Node.html)

### `Parameter Info`

The strongest public description confirms:

- Property ID `636D409`.
- Read/write.
- Type: array of Common Clusters.
- Each array element describes one function item.
- **Element 0 is the return value.**
- Elements 1?�N are the C parameters in order.
- The returned cluster contains only fields relevant to that parameter type; for example, numeric parameters have `Param Passing`, whereas array-specific fields are omitted. [LabVIEW Wiki: Parameter Info](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/CallLibrary_class/Parameter_Info_property)

The available sources support a logical record containing fields equivalent to:

```text
Name
Type
Data Type
Param Passing / Pass By
Array Passing / Array Format
Dimensions
String Format
Constant
Minimum Size
```

But I could not verify a public authoritative table giving the exact LabVIEW 2026 cluster labels, element ordering, physical LabVIEW types, and numeric values for every enum. In particular, the names in the property description are `Param Passing` and `Array Passing`, which may differ from the labels seen in the configuration dialog.

The documented symbolic choices are:

- Parameter type: Numeric, Array, String, Waveform, Digital Waveform, Digital Data, ActiveX, Adapt to Type, and Instance Data Pointer. [LabVIEW external-code description](https://itasdi.uns.ac.rs/pluginfile.php/2260/mod_resource/content/1/LabVIEW_and_Open_Source_Solutions-1.pdf)
- Numeric passing: Value or pointer-to-value. [Same external-code description](https://itasdi.uns.ac.rs/pluginfile.php/2260/mod_resource/content/1/LabVIEW_and_Open_Source_Solutions-1.pdf)
- Array format: Array Data Pointer, Array Handle, Array Handle Pointer.
- String format: C String Pointer, Pascal String Pointer, String Handle, String Handle Pointer.
- Dimensions: integer count.
- Minimum Size: fixed number or reference to a preceding integer parameter; it applies to Array Data Pointer and C String Pointer configurations. [CLFN configuration reference](https://documentation.help/Call-Library-Function-Node/Configuring_the_CLF_Node.html)

For your prototype, the intended symbolic records are approximately:

| Item | Type | Data type | Passing/configuration |
|---|---|---|---|
| `[0]` return | Numeric | Signed 32-bit Integer | Value |
| I32 | Numeric | Signed 32-bit Integer | Value unless prototype says `int32_t *` |
| raw pointer | Numeric | Unsigned 64-bit Integer | Value |
| DBL array | Array | 8-byte Double | Dimensions `1`; Array Data Pointer or Array Handle |
| U8 array | Array | Unsigned 8-bit Integer | Dimensions `1`; required array format |
| C string | String | ??| C String Pointer |
| constant argument | corresponding type | corresponding type | `Constant = TRUE`, if that is actually intended |

For 64-bit LabVIEW, representing an opaque native address as U64 is consistent with community advice to represent opaque pointers as pointer-sized integers. [LAVA discussion by Rolf Kalbermatter](https://lavag.org/topic/16624-release-dll-not-working/)

Important distinction: a U64 containing an address should normally be configured as a numeric U64 **passed by value**. Choosing pointer-to-value would pass the address of LabVIEW?�s U64 storage, producing a `uint64_t *`/pointer-to-pointer-like ABI mismatch.

### Numeric enum values

I found no trustworthy public source for the numeric encodings of:

- Type
- Data Type
- Param Passing
- Array Passing
- String Format

I would not hard-code guessed values. They may be legacy internal enums rather than the visible ring order.

The cheapest reliable check for the manager is:

1. Create one manually configured donor containing every required variant.
2. Read `Parameter Info`.
3. Feed its Common Cluster array to the existing reporter.
4. Report each cluster field?�s name, LabVIEW type descriptor, numeric value, and ring strings.
5. Change one GUI setting at a time and diff the reported cluster.

That produces LabVIEW-2026-specific ground truth without probing undocumented enum ranges or executing the DLL.

## 3. Import Shared Library wizard

NI documents the wizard as a tool that accepts both a shared library and C/C++ header, parses the header, and generates an `.lvlib` containing wrapper VIs with configured CLFNs. [NI: Importing Functions from a Shared Library File](https://www.ni.com/docs/en-AS/bundle/labview/page/importing-functions-from-a-shared-library-file.html)

NI?�s tutorial likewise confirms that the generated wrapper VIs contain preconfigured CLFNs and that the wizard exposes CLFN-like parameter settings. [NI Community tutorial](https://forums.ni.com/t5/Developer-Center-Resources/Tutorial-Creating-Wrapper-VIs-for-C-C-DLL-functions-using-the/ta-p/3522566)

I could not verify from public sources where the LabVIEW 2026 implementation resides. Specifically, I found no reliable citation confirming either:

```text
vi.lib\Utility\...
resource\importwizard\...
```

Nor did I find public evidence of a supported reusable VI named `Update CLFN from prototype` or an API accepting a single C prototype string.

The public CallLibrary method named `Prototype` goes in the opposite direction: it **returns** the C prototype of an already configured node. The class also exposes `Create C File` and an undocumented `Type Only Param` method; none is documented as parsing a prototype into a CLFN. [LabVIEW Wiki: CallLibrary methods](https://labviewwiki.org/wiki/CallLibrary_class)

Therefore:

- **Verified:** the wizard parses header files and generates wrapper VIs.
- **Not verified:** its LabVIEW 2026 installation directory.
- **Not verified:** any supported, reusable prototype-to-CLFN API.
- **Likely inference:** internal implementation VIs may exist but may be private, password-protected, or coupled to wizard state rather than being a stable API.

A plain-text recursive filename listing of the LabVIEW 2026 `resource` and `vi.lib` trees?�not opening any VI?�would settle the location question safely.

## 4. Recommended fallback: donor CLFN templates

A donor VI is the safest route for these signatures:

1. Manually configure one CLFN per distinct ABI/prototype.
2. Copy that node into generated diagrams.
3. Change only `Library Path`, `Function Name`, and, if necessary, `Any Thread?`.
4. Leave `Parameter Info` untouched.
5. Verify the copied node by reading its `Prototype` method and `Parameter Info`.

Copying is explicitly recommended in the NI scripting discussion when construction by style is troublesome. [NI forum](https://forums.ni.com/t5/LabVIEW/VI-Scripting-Input-for-style-in-quot-New-VI-Object-quot-function/td-p/3267356)

I found no documented limitation preventing a configured CLFN from being copied between VIs. The normal caveats are:

- The copied CLFN remains platform- and ABI-dependent; NI says VIs containing CLFNs must reference the appropriate platform library. [NI LabVIEW Development Guidelines](https://download.ni.com/support/manuals/321393d.pdf)
- Library path resolution still applies.
- Pointer size must match LabVIEW/DLL bitness.
- Array Data Pointer and Array Handle are different ABIs and cannot be interchanged merely because both appear as LabVIEW arrays. [CLFN configuration reference](https://documentation.help/Call-Library-Function-Node/Configuring_the_CLF_Node.html)
- ?�Run in any thread??is safe only when the called function and everything it invokes are thread-safe. [CLFN threading guidance](https://documentation.help/Call-Library-Function-Node/Configuring_the_CLF_Node.html)

My recommendation is therefore: **use direct creation only to establish that `Call Library Function Node` works in LabVIEW 2026, but use donor-node copying for production.** Treat fully synthesized `Parameter Info` as an optional later optimization after the manager captures the exact 2026 cluster schema and enum values from reporter output.
